{
    "background_story": "Kenzo was a young and ambitious designer who idolized the works of Marcel Breuer, a renowned architect and furniture designer. Growing up in a family of artists, Kenzo was exposed to the Bauhaus movement from a young age and was particularly drawn to Breuer's innovative use of tubular steel. After completing his studies in industrial design, Kenzo landed an internship at a prestigious design firm, where he worked tirelessly to hone his skills. His big break came when he was commissioned to design a chair for a high-end furniture brand. Inspired by Breuer's Wassily Chair, Kenzo created a sleek and modern piece that paid homage to his idol while showcasing his own unique style. The chair became an instant hit, and Kenzo's career as a designer was launched. He continued to draw inspiration from Breuer's work, always pushing the boundaries of modern design.",
